/* ===================== WEEK 1 ===================== */
C({ id: 'decision', week: 1, title: 'Single-agent decisions vs strategic interaction', pri: 'Supporting',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.1', text: '✓ Ch.2 §1 (decisions vs games)', lec: '✓ Intro lecture' }, prereq: [],
  misShort: 'Treating a strategic situation as if the other side’s choice were a fixed parameter.',
  intu: R`<p>When you alone choose what to eat, your satisfaction depends only on <em>your</em> choice: list the alternatives, value each, pick the best. That is <b>decision theory</b>. The moment your payoff also depends on what somebody else chooses — and their payoff depends on yours — you can no longer simply “pick the best”: what is best depends on what they do, and what they do depends on what they expect you to do. That circular dependence is a <b>game</b>.</p>
  <p>The course’s running example: eating out. Alone, a cheap dish (value 1000, price 500) beats an expensive one (value 2500, price 3000). Split the bill with a friend and the same menu becomes a strategic problem — your order raises <em>their</em> bill.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>It is the reason game theory exists: optimisation tools for one decision-maker (maximise $u(a)$) fail when $u$ depends on another agent’s choice.</li><li>Every later concept — best response, Nash equilibrium — is “single-agent optimisation, holding the others’ choices fixed”, applied to every player at once.</li><li>Recognising parametric vs strategic settings tells you which toolkit to use.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p><b>Decision problem:</b> a set of alternatives $A$, an outcome map $A\to O$, and a preference (or utility $u:O\to\mathbb R$). The agent chooses $a^*\in\arg\max_{a\in A}u(a)$.</p><p><b>Strategic interaction (game):</b> each agent $i$ chooses $a_i\in A_i$ and receives $u_i(a_1,\dots,a_n)$ — a payoff that depends on <em>everyone’s</em> actions.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Parametric optimisation</b> treats the environment as fixed: $\max_{a}u(a;\theta)$ with $\theta$ given by nature. <b>Strategic optimisation</b> replaces $\theta$ by other players’ choices $a_{-i}$, which are themselves chosen optimally: $\max_{a_i}u_i(a_i,a_{-i})$ where $a_{-i}$ responds to $a_i$’s anticipation. The value of an item is “monetised” through the indifference price — the price at which you would be indifferent between buying and not buying.</p>
  <p><b>Discrete allocation (marginal reasoning).</b> When a resource (e.g. study hours) is allocated across uses, compare marginal gains: give each additional unit to the use with the highest marginal gain (valid when marginal gains are decreasing).</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Eating out alone', q: R`<p>Cheap dish: value 1000, price 500. Expensive dish: value 2500, price 3000. Which do you order alone?</p>`, s: R`<p>Net gain = value − price. Cheap: $1000-500=+500$. Expensive: $2500-3000=-500$. Order cheap. Nothing depends on anyone else — a pure decision problem.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Splitting the bill turns it into a game', q: R`<p>Two diners each choose Cheap (C) or Expensive (E); the total bill is split 50–50. Build the payoff matrix and say what each should order.</p>`, s: R`<p>Each pays half the total. $(C,C)$: bill 1000, each pays 500 → $(500,500)$. $(C,E)$: bill 3500, each pays 1750 → you get $1000-1750=-750$, friend $2500-1750=750$. $(E,C)$ symmetric $(750,-750)$. $(E,E)$: each pays 3000 → $(-500,-500)$.</p>${M.mat({ rows: ['C', 'E'], cols: ['C', 'E'], cells: [['500, 500', '−750, 750'], ['750, −750', '−500, −500']], rp: 'You', cp: 'Friend' })}<p>Against C, E gives 750 > 500; against E, E gives −500 > −750. E is better whatever the friend does (strictly dominant), so both order E and get −500 each, although (C,C) gives both +500. Splitting the bill creates a Prisoner’s-Dilemma structure.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Allocating study hours (course assignment theme)', q: R`<p>Marks (out of 10) by hours studied — Economics: 0h 7.0, 1h 7.7, 2h 8.2, 3h 8.5; Geography: 0h 6.0, 1h 6.8, 2h 7.4, 3h 7.8. Hours are indivisible. Maximise total marks with (a) 1 hour, (b) 2 hours, (c) 3 hours.</p>`, s: R`<p>Marginal gains — Econ: +0.7, +0.5, +0.3; Geog: +0.8, +0.6, +0.4. Both are decreasing, so allocate greedily.</p><ol><li>1 hour: Geog (+0.8) beats Econ (+0.7) → (E,G)=(0,1), total 13.8.</li><li>2 hours: second hour: Econ +0.7 vs Geog +0.6 → (1,1), total 14.5. Check: (0,2)=14.4, (2,0)=14.2.</li><li>3 hours: third hour: Geog +0.6 vs Econ +0.5 → (1,2), total 7.7+7.4=15.1. Check (2,1)=15.0, (0,3)=14.8, (3,0)=14.5.</li></ol><p>This is single-agent optimisation: no other agent’s choice enters your payoff.</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['C', 'E'], cols: ['C', 'E'], cells: [['500, 500', '−750, 750'], ['750, −750', '−500, −500']], rp: 'You', cp: 'Friend', br: true, ne: 'auto', cap: 'Underlined = best response; green = both best-responding. The bill-splitting game.' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Leads to <a href="#/c/prefs">preferences &amp; utility</a> (how values are represented), <a href="#/c/game-elements">elements of a game</a>, and the <a href="#/c/pd">Prisoner’s Dilemma</a> (the bill-splitting game is one). The “fix others, optimise yourself” idea becomes <a href="#/c/best-response">best response</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li><b>“Game” means conflict.</b> No — any interdependence of payoffs makes a game, even with common interests.</li><li><b>Optimising against a fixed belief = solving the game.</b> In a game the belief must itself be consistent with the other player’s optimisation (that is what equilibrium adds).</li><li><b>Greedy allocation always works.</b> Only when marginal gains are non-increasing; otherwise check all splits.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<ul><li>Definition-style MCQ: “what makes a situation strategic?” — interdependent payoffs.</li><li>Small numeric optimisation items (course assignments) — compute marginal gains, verify by enumeration.</li><li>No supplied quiz PYQ tests this directly; it is supporting knowledge.</li></ul>`)}` });

C({ id: 'prefs', week: 1, title: 'Preferences, rationality axioms & utility', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.7.1', text: '✓ Ch.2 §3', lec: '✓ Intro lecture (axioms)' }, prereq: ['decision'],
  misShort: 'Confusing completeness (can compare any pair) with transitivity (consistency across three).',
  intu: R`<p>Before we can say a player “maximises payoff”, the player must be able to rank outcomes sensibly. Two requirements make ranking possible: you can always compare two outcomes (<b>completeness</b>), and your comparisons don’t go round in circles (<b>transitivity</b>). If someone prefers tea to coffee, coffee to lassi, and lassi to tea, there is no “best” drink — and a trader could exploit them forever by selling the swaps.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Complete and transitive preferences (on a finite set) can be represented by a utility function: $x\succsim y\iff u(x)\ge u(y)$. Payoff numbers in every matrix are such utilities.</li><li>Most of Weeks 1–2 needs only the <em>ordering</em> (ordinal payoffs); mixed strategies (Week 3) need expected utility, i.e. cardinal meaning.</li><li>Rationality in game theory = consistent preferences + maximising them.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>A preference relation $\succsim$ on outcomes $O$ (“at least as good as”), with strict part $\succ$ and indifference $\sim$:</p><ul><li><b>Completeness:</b> for all $x,y\in O$: $x\succsim y$ or $y\succsim x$ (or both). Exactly one of $x\succ y$, $y\succ x$, $x\sim y$ holds.</li><li><b>Transitivity:</b> for all $x,y,z$: $x\succsim y$ and $y\succsim z\Rightarrow x\succsim z$.</li></ul><p>A <b>utility function</b> $u:O\to\mathbb R$ represents $\succsim$ if $x\succsim y \iff u(x)\ge u(y)$.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Ordinal invariance.</b> If $u$ represents $\succsim$ and $f$ is strictly increasing, then $f\circ u$ also represents $\succsim$. Hence in pure-strategy analysis (dominance, pure Nash equilibrium) you may replace payoffs by any numbers with the same ranking for each player — e.g. 1, 2, 3 for worst, middle, best. Equilibria do not change.</p><p><b>Cardinal (expected) utility</b> is needed once lotteries enter: the value of a lottery $(p,x;1-p,y)$ is $p\,u(x)+(1-p)\,u(y)$; then only positive affine transformations $a u+b$ ($a>0$) preserve choices.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Which axiom fails?', q: R`<p>(i) A shopper cannot compare two phones at all. (ii) Someone prefers tea ≻ coffee, coffee ≻ lassi, lassi ≻ tea.</p>`, s: R`<p>(i) violates <b>completeness</b> (no ranking exists). (ii) violates <b>transitivity</b> (a cycle). Both appear in the Week-1 course assignment.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Ordinal payoffs for the War Game', q: R`<p>Country 1 ranks: capture island without a fight ≻ no attack ≻ fight. Assign ordinal payoffs and show any increasing relabelling gives the same choice.</p>`, s: R`<p>Use 3, 2, 1 (or 10, 5, 0 as in lectures). The choice maximises payoff; replacing 1,2,3 by 0,5,10 preserves every comparison, so the optimal action is unchanged.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Ordinal vs cardinal', q: R`<p>Row payoffs 1, 2, 4 for outcomes x, y, z. A lottery gives x or z with probability ½ each. Compare it with y. Then replace payoffs by 1, 2, 3 (same ranking). Does the comparison survive?</p>`, s: R`<p>With 1,2,4: lottery $=\tfrac12(1)+\tfrac12(4)=2.5>2$ → prefer lottery. With 1,2,3: $=2=2$ → indifferent. The <em>ranking</em> of sure outcomes is identical, yet the lottery comparison changed: lotteries require cardinal utility. So mixed-strategy answers (Week 3) depend on the actual numbers, pure-strategy answers do not.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Feeds <a href="#/c/assumptions">rationality assumption</a>, <a href="#/c/game-elements">payoff functions</a>, and later <a href="#/c/mixed-strategy">expected utility under mixing</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Reflexivity/convexity are not the axioms emphasised in this course — the two named are completeness and transitivity.</li><li>Payoffs in a matrix need not be money; they are utilities (altruism can be built in).</li><li>“Indifference violates completeness” — no: indifference $x\sim y$ <em>is</em> a comparison.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Expect one-line MCQs mapping a story to the violated axiom (course assignment style). Key words: “cannot compare / confused” → completeness; “cycle” → transitivity.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'game-elements', week: 1, title: 'Elements of a game & formal notation', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.2', text: '✓ Ch.2 §3', lec: '✓ W1_L1, L4' }, prereq: ['decision'],
  misShort: 'Forgetting that a payoff function takes the whole action profile as input.',
  intu: R`<p>To describe any strategic situation you answer: <b>Who</b> is playing? <b>What</b> can each do? <b>When</b> and with what <b>information</b>? What does each <b>get</b> for every combination of choices? A model abstracts — like a railway map that shows stations but not every tree — keeping only what matters for strategy.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Every quiz question begins with “formulate the game” — get the players, actions and payoffs right and the rest is mechanical.</li><li>The deleted-profile notation $a_{-i}$ is used in every definition of dominance, best response and Nash equilibrium.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>A finite <b>strategic-form game</b> is $G=\langle N,(A_i)_{i\in N},(u_i)_{i\in N}\rangle$ with players $N=\{1,\dots,n\}$, action sets $A_i$, and payoff functions $u_i:A\to\mathbb R$ where $A=A_1\times\cdots\times A_n$ is the set of action profiles.</p><p><b>Deleted profile:</b> $a_{-i}=(a_1,\dots,a_{i-1},a_{i+1},\dots,a_n)\in A_{-i}=\prod_{j\ne i}A_j$, and we write $a=(a_i,a_{-i})$.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Counting.</b> If player $i$ has $k_i$ actions, there are $|A|=\prod_i k_i$ profiles, and each $u_i$ maps that many profiles to one real number (domain size $\prod_i k_i$, range $\mathbb R$). A 2-player game with 3 and 4 actions has 12 cells, each with 2 payoffs.</p><p><b>Rules of the game</b> (course list): players; actions; payoffs; timing (simultaneous or sequential); nature of interaction (once or repeated); information (who knows what); enforceability of agreements.</p><p><b>Modelling notes from lectures:</b> utilities can include altruism (if Ranga cares about Billa’s sentence, add it to Ranga’s payoff); <b>mechanism design</b> runs game theory in reverse — choose rules so that rational play produces a desired outcome (e.g. auction design).</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Identify the elements', q: R`<p>Two drivers at a junction each Follow rules (F) or Defect (D). Payoffs: (F,F)=(3,3), (F,D)=(0,4), (D,F)=(4,0), (D,D)=(1,1). State $N$, $A_i$, $u_1(D,F)$.</p>`, s: R`<p>$N=\{1,2\}$, $A_1=A_2=\{F,D\}$, $u_1(D,F)=4$. There are $2\times2=4$ profiles.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Deleted profiles in a 3-player game', q: R`<p>Players 1, 2, 3 with $A_1=\{U,D\}$, $A_2=\{L,R\}$, $A_3=\{A,B\}$. List $A_{-2}$ and write the profile where 1 plays D, 2 plays L, 3 plays B as $(a_2,a_{-2})$.</p>`, s: R`<p>$A_{-2}=A_1\times A_3=\{(U,A),(U,B),(D,A),(D,B)\}$. The profile is $(L,(D,B))$. A 2×2×2 game is drawn as two 2×2 matrices, one for each action of player 3.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Formulating the auction', q: R`<p>Formulate the PYQ auction (values 3 and 5; bids 0,1,2; higher bid wins and pays own bid; tie → coin toss, winner pays) as $\langle N,(A_i),(u_i)\rangle$.</p>`, s: R`<p>$N=\{1,2\}$, $A_i=\{0,1,2\}$, $u_i(b_i,b_j)=v_i-b_i$ if $b_i>b_j$; $\tfrac12(v_i-b_i)$ if $b_i=b_j$; $0$ if $b_i<b_j$, with $v_1=3$, $v_2=5$. The ½ is the expected value of the coin toss (expected-utility payoff). See <a href="#/c/dominance">dominance</a> for the full matrix.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Representation: <a href="#/c/normal-form">strategic form</a> and <a href="#/c/tree">extensive form</a>. Notation reused in <a href="#/c/dominance">dominance</a>, <a href="#/c/best-response">best response</a>, <a href="#/c/nash">Nash equilibrium</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>$a_{-i}$ is not “the opponent’s action” in an $n$-player game — it is the whole profile of everyone except $i$.</li><li>Payoff domain is the set of <em>profiles</em> $A$, not $A_i$.</li><li>Inspector Vinod in the Prisoner’s Dilemma is <em>not</em> a player: he makes no strategic choice in the model.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Comprehension blocks begin “Formulate the game in matrix form…”. Every PYQ in this dashboard depends on correct formulation, especially tie rules (auction) and payoff orientation (row player first).</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'normal-form', week: 1, title: 'Strategic (normal) form representation', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.4', text: '✓ Ch.4 §1', lec: '✓ W1_L4' }, prereq: ['game-elements'],
  misShort: 'Reading the column player’s payoff as the first number in a cell.',
  intu: R`<p>A strategic form is a table: rows are player 1’s actions, columns are player 2’s, and each cell lists what each gets. It strips away timing — useful when players choose without seeing each other’s choice.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>The matrix is where you apply dominance, underline best responses and spot Nash equilibria.</li><li>Every extensive-form game can be converted into one (Week 4), which is how “how many Nash equilibria” questions on trees are answered.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>For two players with $A_1=\{r_1,\dots,r_m\}$ and $A_2=\{c_1,\dots,c_n\}$, the strategic form is the $m\times n$ table whose $(r,c)$ cell is $(u_1(r,c),u_2(r,c))$. Convention: <b>row player’s payoff first</b>. With three players, use one matrix per action of player 3.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p>Reading rules: <b>player 1 compares down a column</b> (fix $c$, vary $r$, look at first numbers); <b>player 2 compares along a row</b> (fix $r$, vary $c$, look at second numbers). Constant-sum check: $u_1+u_2$ the same in every cell.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Prisoner’s Dilemma table', q: R`<p>Both confess: 5 years each; one confesses: confessor 0, other 20; both deny: 1 year each. Write the matrix with payoffs = −years.</p>`, s: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['Confess', 'Deny'], cols: ['Confess', 'Deny'], cells: [['−5, −5', '0, −20'], ['−20, 0', '−1, −1']], rp: 'Ranga', cp: 'Billa' })}` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Building the police–dealer table', q: R`<p>100 potential trades. The PYQ table gives (Police, Dealer) = (80,20), (0,100) in row Street; (10,90), (60,40) in row Park; columns Dealer Street, Park. Verify it is constant-sum and interpret one cell.</p>`, s: R`<p>Each cell sums to 100 → constant-sum. (Street, Park)=(0,100): police watch the street, dealer is in the park → no trade is disrupted, all 100 happen.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Auction matrix with tie rule', q: R`<p>Build the 3×3 matrix for values $v_1=3$, $v_2=5$, bids {0,1,2}, coin-toss ties.</p>`, s: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['b₁=0', 'b₁=1', 'b₁=2'], cols: ['b₂=0', 'b₂=1', 'b₂=2'], cells: [['1.5, 2.5', '0, 4', '0, 3'], ['2, 0', '1, 2', '0, 3'], ['1, 0', '1, 0', '0.5, 1.5']] })}<p>Diagonal = ties: $\tfrac12(v_i-b)$. E.g. (1,1): $u_1=\tfrac12(3-1)=1$, $u_2=\tfrac12(5-1)=2$.</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['U', 'D'], cols: ['L', 'R'], cells: [['u₁(U,L), u₂(U,L)', 'u₁(U,R), u₂(U,R)'], ['u₁(D,L), u₂(D,L)', 'u₁(D,R), u₂(D,R)']], cap: 'Anatomy of a cell: row player’s payoff first.' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Used throughout <a href="#/c/dominance">dominance</a>, <a href="#/c/nash">Nash equilibrium</a>, <a href="#/c/indifference">mixed equilibrium</a>; built from trees in <a href="#/c/ef-to-nf">normal form of an extensive game</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Swapping payoff order (most frequent arithmetic slip in PYQs).</li><li>Thinking normal form implies players literally move at the same instant — it means neither observes the other’s choice.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Many PYQs give the matrix as an image/table (drug dealer, 2×2 numeric games). Copy it carefully and label rows/columns before computing.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'classify', week: 1, title: 'Classifying games (static/dynamic, information)', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.4', text: '✓ Ch.2 §2', lec: '✓ W1_L2, L3, L5' }, prereq: ['game-elements'],
  misShort: 'Equating “simultaneous” with “at the same clock time” or perfect with complete information.',
  intu: R`<p>Games differ in <em>timing</em> (do later movers see earlier moves?) and <em>information</em> (does everyone know the payoffs?). Classifying first tells you which representation and solution tool to use: matrices and Nash for static games; trees and backward induction for dynamic ones; beliefs over types for incomplete information.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>PYQs ask “This is a game of perfect / imperfect information”.</li><li>The 2×2 taxonomy organises the whole course: Weeks 1–3 static complete-information; Week 4 dynamic complete information; later weeks incomplete information.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<ul><li><b>Static (simultaneous)</b>: each player chooses without observing others’ choices. <b>Dynamic (sequential)</b>: some players observe earlier moves.</li><li><b>Complete information</b>: players, actions and payoff functions are common knowledge. <b>Incomplete</b>: someone has private information (e.g. their valuation) — a Bayesian game.</li><li><b>Perfect information</b> (dynamic games): every information set is a single node — each mover knows the entire history. <b>Imperfect</b>: some information set contains ≥2 nodes.</li><li>Other dichotomies (course notes): symmetric/asymmetric, zero-sum/non-zero-sum, finite/infinite, discrete/continuous, deterministic/stochastic, cooperative/non-cooperative.</li></ul>`)}`,
  math: R`<table class="t"><tr><th></th><th>Static</th><th>Dynamic</th></tr><tr><th>Complete info</th><td>Normal form, complete info (Weeks 1–3)</td><td>Extensive form, complete info (Week 4)</td></tr><tr><th>Incomplete info</th><td>Bayesian normal form</td><td>Dynamic Bayesian games</td></tr></table>
  <p><b>Adverse selection (takeover) example.</b> Target value $x$ uniform on $\{0,\dots,100\}$, worth $1.5x$ to acquirer; target accepts $y$ iff $y\ge x$. With offer $y$, acceptance happens exactly when $x\le y$, so $E[x\mid x\le y]=y/2$ and the acquirer’s expected gain given acceptance is $1.5\cdot\tfrac y2-y=-\tfrac y4<0$. Any positive bid loses money on average: the best bid is 0. Private information (incomplete information) destroys a deal that complete information would make.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Classify', q: R`<p>Classify: (a) chess, (b) sealed-bid auction where values are known, (c) poker.</p>`, s: R`<p>(a) dynamic, perfect information. (b) static, complete information. (c) dynamic, imperfect and incomplete (private cards).</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Complete-information takeover', q: R`<p>Target value $x\in\{50,100\}$ known to <em>both</em>. Offers $y\in\{0,25,50,75\}$; target accepts iff $y\ge x$. What happens when $x=50$?</p>`, s: R`<p>Acquirer values the firm at 75; target accepts any $y\ge50$, so offer 50 → acquirer gets $75-50=25$, target 50. With complete information a mutually beneficial trade occurs.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Incomplete information destroys trade', q: R`<p>Same, but only the target knows $x\in\{50,100\}$ (each prob. ½). Compute the acquirer’s expected payoff from offers 50 and 75.</p>`, s: R`<p>Offer 50: accepted only if $x=50$ → $\tfrac12(75-50)+\tfrac12\cdot0=12.5$. Offer 75: also accepted only if $x=50$ (75 < 100) → $\tfrac12(75-75)=0$. Offer 0, 25: rejected → 0. Best: 50, expected 12.5, and trade fails half the time although the firm is always worth more to the acquirer.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p><a href="#/c/info-sets">Information sets</a> make “perfect vs imperfect” precise; <a href="#/c/zero-sum">zero/constant-sum</a> games appear in Week 3; <a href="#/c/coop">cooperative games</a> are the other branch.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li><b>Perfect ≠ complete information.</b> Perfect: observe past moves. Complete: know payoffs. A game can be complete but imperfect (simultaneous moves).</li><li>“Simultaneous” means no observation of others’ choices, not identical timing.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Every A/BC/DE/F tree PYQ opens with “This is a game of: Perfect information / Imperfect …”. All decision nodes are singletons → perfect information.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'coop', week: 1, title: 'Non-cooperative vs cooperative games', pri: 'Supporting',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.4', text: '✓ Ch.2 §2', lec: '✓ W1_L3, L7' }, prereq: ['classify'],
  misShort: '“Non-cooperative” does not mean players are hostile; it means no binding agreements.',
  intu: R`<p>Non-cooperative theory models each individual’s procedure — moves, information, incentives — and asks what each will do. Cooperative theory takes binding agreements as available and asks which <em>coalitions</em> form and how they split what they can jointly earn.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Quiz 1 is entirely non-cooperative; but definition questions (course assignments) test the distinction and coalition counting.</li><li>Characteristic functions reappear in later weeks (Shapley value).</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p><b>Non-cooperative:</b> unit of analysis = player; agreements not binding unless enforced inside the game. <b>Cooperative (coalitional):</b> unit = coalition $S\subseteq N$; a <b>characteristic function</b> $v(S)$ gives the total a coalition can secure. <b>TU</b> (transferable utility): $v(S)$ can be split freely by side payments; <b>NTU</b>: not.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p>With $n$ players there are $2^n$ subsets of $N$, of which $2^n-1$ are non-empty coalitions. For $n=4$: $2^4=16$ subsets but <b>15</b> non-empty coalitions. Marginal contribution of $i$ to $S$: $v(S\cup\{i\})-v(S)$.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Counting coalitions', q: R`<p>How many non-empty coalitions with 3 players? With 4?</p>`, s: R`<p>$2^3-1=7$; $2^4-1=15$. (A course assignment states “16 coalitions with 4 players”; this is <b>false</b> if the empty set is not counted as a coalition.)</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Glove (matching shoes) game', q: R`<p>Player 1 has a right shoe, players 2 and 3 a left shoe each; a pair is worth 1. Find $v(S)$ for all $S$.</p>`, s: R`<p>Singletons: 0. $v(\{1,2\})=v(\{1,3\})=1$, $v(\{2,3\})=0$, $v(\{1,2,3\})=1$. Player 3’s marginal contribution to $\{1,2\}$ is $1-1=0$, so 2 and 3 compete for player 1 — the scarce right shoe holds the power.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Which framework?', q: R`<p>Firms can sign court-enforceable cartel contracts. Which framework is natural, and how would a non-cooperative theorist still model it?</p>`, s: R`<p>Cooperative (binding agreements, coalition formation). A non-cooperative modeller would add “sign contract”, “breach” and “sue” as moves with legal penalties in the payoffs — enforcement becomes part of the game.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Contrasts with everything else in Weeks 1–4 (non-cooperative). The Prisoner’s Dilemma shows why binding agreements matter.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Non-cooperative games can have perfectly aligned interests (coordination games).</li><li>Forgetting the empty set when counting subsets vs coalitions.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Possible definition MCQ or true/false about coalition counts. Not tested in supplied Quiz-1 PYQs.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'pd', week: 1, title: 'The Prisoner’s Dilemma', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.5', text: '✓ Ch.4 §3', lec: '✓ W1_L4, W2_L2b' }, prereq: ['normal-form'],
  misShort: 'Thinking the PD equilibrium is efficient, or that the dilemma needs both players to be selfish in money terms.',
  intu: R`<p>Two suspects are interrogated separately. Whatever the other does, confessing gets each a shorter sentence — yet if both stay silent both are better off than if both confess. Individually rational choices produce a collectively bad result. This tension between private incentive and joint welfare is the single most important 2×2 game.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>The canonical example of a strictly dominant strategy equilibrium that is Pareto-inefficient.</li><li>Explains price wars, arms races, over-ordering when splitting bills, over-fishing.</li><li>Shows why enforceable agreements or repetition matter.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>Symmetric 2×2 game with cooperate C / defect D and payoffs (row): $u(C,C)=R$, $u(C,D)=S$, $u(D,C)=T$, $u(D,D)=P$ is a <b>Prisoner’s Dilemma</b> iff $$T>R>P>S.$$ (Temptation > Reward > Punishment > Sucker.)</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Why the inequalities.</b> (i) D strictly dominates C: against C, $T>R$; against D, $P>S$. (ii) The dominant-strategy outcome (D,D) is worse for both than (C,C): $R>P$. Combining: $T>R>P>S$.</p><p>Lecture labelling: cells $(C,C)=(a,a)$, $(C,D)=(c,b)$, $(D,C)=(b,c)$, $(D,D)=(d,d)$ give $b>a>d>c$ — the same condition with different letters.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Ranga and Billa', q: R`<p>Show Confess is strictly dominant in the course PD.</p>`, s: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['C', 'D'], cols: ['C', 'D'], cells: [['−5, −5', '0, −20'], ['−20, 0', '−1, −1']], rp: 'Ranga', cp: 'Billa', br: true, ne: 'auto' })}<p>Ranga: vs C, $-5>-20$; vs D, $0>-1$ → C dominant. Same for Billa. Equilibrium (C,C) with (−5,−5), though (D,D) gives (−1,−1).</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Is it a PD?', q: R`<p>Row payoffs: $(C,C)=3$, $(C,D)=0$, $(D,C)=4$, $(D,D)=1$. Is this a PD? The traffic game of lecture 1 uses these numbers.</p>`, s: R`<p>$T=4>R=3>P=1>S=0$ ✓ — yes, the traffic game is a PD: Defect is dominant, (D,D) gives (1,1) < (3,3).</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Parameter range', q: R`<p>Row payoffs $R=6$, $S=0$, $P=2$, $T=x$. For which $x$ is the game a PD? For which is (C,C) a Nash equilibrium?</p>`, s: R`<p>PD needs $x>6$ (and $6>2>0$ holds). If $x<6$, C is a best reply to C (6 > x) while D is the best reply to D (2 > 0): two pure NE (C,C) and (D,D) — a Stag Hunt. At $x=6$ both C and D are best replies to C. So (C,C) is NE iff $x\le6$.</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['C', 'D'], cols: ['C', 'D'], cells: [['R, R', 'S, T'], ['T, S', 'P, P']], cap: 'General PD: T > R > P > S.' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Example of <a href="#/c/dominance">strict dominance</a> and <a href="#/c/dse">dominant-strategy equilibrium</a>; contrast with <a href="#/c/classic-games">Stag Hunt, BoS, Hawk–Dove</a>. Sequential PD in <a href="#/c/ef-strategy">Week 4</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>The equilibrium is unique and in dominant strategies — no need for beliefs.</li><li>“Players would cooperate if they talked” — non-binding talk cannot change a dominant strategy.</li><li>Altruism does not “break” game theory; it changes payoffs and possibly removes the dilemma.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Expect identification of the PD inequality and of dominant strategies; the Week-2 course assignment asks for best responses and the NE of a PD (Defect, Defect).</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'dominance', week: 1, title: 'Strict & weak dominance; dominant strategies', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.6', text: '✓ Ch.4 §3', lec: '✓ W1_L6, W2_L1a' }, prereq: ['normal-form'],
  misShort: 'Calling a strategy strictly dominated when it ties in some column (that is at most weak dominance).',
  intu: R`<p>One option <b>dominates</b> another if it is better no matter what others do. If it is always strictly better, a rational player will never use the worse one. If it is at least as good always and strictly better sometimes, it <b>weakly</b> dominates. A <b>dominant strategy</b> dominates <em>every</em> other strategy — you don’t need to predict anyone.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>The first and most robust solution idea: needs only your own rationality.</li><li>The basis of iterated elimination (IESDS) — tested in every auction PYQ.</li><li>PYQ: “Which player does not have a strictly dominated strategy?”</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>For player $i$ and $a_i,a_i'\in A_i$:</p><ul><li>$a_i$ <b>strictly dominates</b> $a_i'$ if $u_i(a_i,a_{-i})>u_i(a_i',a_{-i})$ for all $a_{-i}\in A_{-i}$.</li><li>$a_i$ <b>weakly dominates</b> $a_i'$ if $u_i(a_i,a_{-i})\ge u_i(a_i',a_{-i})$ for all $a_{-i}$, with strict inequality for at least one $a_{-i}$.</li><li>$a_i$ is <b>strictly (weakly) dominant</b> if it strictly (weakly) dominates every other action of $i$.</li></ul>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Procedure for a pair</b> (row player): compare the two rows entry by entry in the row player’s payoffs. All “>” → strict. All “≥” with at least one “>” → weak. Any “<” somewhere → neither dominates. For the column player compare columns using second entries.</p><p><b>Quantifiers matter.</b> Strict: $\forall a_{-i}$. Weak: $\forall a_{-i}$ with $\ge$ and $\exists a_{-i}^*$ with $>$.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Spot dominance', q: R`<p>Row payoffs: U = (3, 2), D = (1, 2) against L, R. Relationship?</p>`, s: R`<p>U vs D: $3>1$, $2=2$ → U <b>weakly</b> dominates D, not strictly.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Second-price auction (truthful bidding)', q: R`<p>Why is bidding your value weakly — not strictly — dominant in a second-price auction?</p>`, s: R`<p>Bidding $v$ never does worse than any other bid, and does strictly better against some rival bids (where a different bid would change the winner unprofitably). Against many rival bids, a different bid yields the same outcome → ties → only weak dominance.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'The PYQ auction (values 3, 5)', q: R`<p>Using the auction matrix (coin-toss ties), which player has a strictly dominated pure strategy?</p>`, s: R`<p>Player 2’s payoffs by column against $b_1=0,1,2$: $b_2=0:(2.5,0,0)$, $b_2=1:(4,2,0)$, $b_2=2:(3,3,1.5)$. $b_2=2$ vs $b_2=0$: $3>2.5$, $3>0$, $1.5>0$ → <b>$b_2=0$ strictly dominated</b>.</p><p>Player 1’s rows: $b_1=0:(1.5,0,0)$, $b_1=1:(2,1,0)$, $b_1=2:(1,1,0.5)$. Pairs: 1 vs 0 ties at the last column (0=0); 2 vs 0 loses in column 1 ($1<1.5$); 1 vs 2 and 2 vs 1 each lose somewhere. No pure strategy of player 1 is strictly dominated by another pure strategy → the official answer “Player 1 does not have a strictly dominated strategy”. (Subtlety: $b_1=0$ <em>is</em> strictly dominated by the mixture $0.75\cdot b_1{=}1+0.25\cdot b_1{=}2$ — see <a href="#/c/mixed-dom">mixed dominance</a>. The quiz key uses pure-strategy domination.)</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['b₁=0', 'b₁=1', 'b₁=2'], cols: ['b₂=0', 'b₂=1', 'b₂=2'], cells: [['1.5, 2.5', '0, 4', '0, 3'], ['2, 0', '1, 2', '0, 3'], ['1, 0', '1, 0', '0.5, 1.5']], hl: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0]], cap: 'Shaded column b₂=0 is strictly dominated by b₂=2 for player 2 (compare second entries).' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Builds <a href="#/c/dse">dominant-strategy equilibrium</a>, <a href="#/c/iesds">IESDS</a>, <a href="#/c/iewds">IEWDS</a>; extended by <a href="#/c/mixed-dom">domination by mixed strategies</a> and <a href="#/c/nbr">never-best-response</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>“Dominates” vs “dominant”: an action can dominate one other action without being dominant.</li><li>A tie in a single column kills strict dominance.</li><li>Checking the wrong player’s payoffs (column player uses the <em>second</em> number).</li><li>Answer “Player 1” to “which player has <em>no</em> strictly dominated strategy” — read the negation carefully.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Auction family appears in 4 of 5 papers (values 3/5 or 5/4). Recipe: build matrix with the tie rule → compare pairs for each player → note which player has none.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'mixed-dom', week: 1, title: 'Mixed actions & domination by a mixed strategy', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.6.1, §13.2', text: '✓ Ch.7 (mixed)', lec: '✓ W1_L6, W3_L4a' }, prereq: ['dominance'],
  misShort: 'Concluding “no dominated strategy” after checking only pure strategies.',
  intu: R`<p>A strategy that is never beaten by any single alternative can still be beaten by a <em>blend</em> of two alternatives. If U is great against L and bad against R, and M is the reverse, then flipping a coin between U and M gives a decent payoff against both — possibly better than a “safe middle” option D everywhere.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Lectures stress: in a 3×2 matrix with no pure domination, check mixtures before concluding nothing is dominated.</li><li>Needed to run IESDS fully and for the never-best-response theorem (Week 3).</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>A <b>mixed action</b> $\sigma_i\in\Delta(A_i)$ is a probability distribution over $A_i$: $\sigma_i(a)\ge0$, $\sum_a\sigma_i(a)=1$. Its payoff against $a_{-i}$ is $U_i(\sigma_i,a_{-i})=\sum_{a\in A_i}\sigma_i(a)u_i(a,a_{-i})$. Pure $a_i'$ is <b>strictly dominated by $\sigma_i$</b> if $U_i(\sigma_i,a_{-i})>u_i(a_i',a_{-i})$ for all $a_{-i}$.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Two-strategy test.</b> To check whether $b=(b_L,b_R)$ is dominated by mixing $x=(x_L,x_R)$ (weight $\alpha$) and $y=(y_L,y_R)$: solve $\alpha x_L+(1-\alpha)y_L>b_L$ and $\alpha x_R+(1-\alpha)y_R>b_R$ for $\alpha\in[0,1]$. A non-empty interval of $\alpha$ ⇒ dominated.</p><p><b>Support rule.</b> A mixture can never strictly dominate <em>all</em> the actions in its own support at once (an average can’t exceed every one of its components). So when testing whether $b$ is dominated, mix actions other than $b$.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Lecture 3×2 example', q: R`<p>Row payoffs vs L, R: U=(3,0), M=(0,3), D=(1,1). Is D dominated?</p>`, s: R`<p>No pure row dominates D (U loses vs R, M loses vs L). Mix ½U+½M: (1.5, 1.5) > (1, 1) in both columns → D strictly dominated by the mixture. Range: $3\alpha>1$ and $3(1-\alpha)>1$ ⇒ $\alpha\in(1/3,2/3)$.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'BoS variant with a third action X (illustrative numbers)', q: R`<p>P2’s payoffs against P1’s B and S: column B = (3,0), column S = (0,4), column X = (1,1). Is X dominated?</p>`, s: R`<p>Mix $\beta B+(1-\beta)S$: against B: $3\beta>1\Rightarrow\beta>1/3$; against S: $4(1-\beta)>1\Rightarrow\beta<3/4$. Any $\beta\in(1/3,3/4)$, e.g. ½ giving (1.5, 2) > (1, 1). X is strictly dominated. The Week-1 course assignment asks the same question about its own BoS variant; its matrix is only legible as an image, so these numbers are illustrative.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Hidden domination in the auction', q: R`<p>In the auction (values 3, 5), is $b_1=0$ = (1.5, 0, 0) strictly dominated by a mixture of $b_1=1$ = (2, 1, 0) and $b_1=2$ = (1, 1, 0.5)?</p>`, s: R`<p>Weight $\alpha$ on $b_1=1$: column 0: $2\alpha+(1-\alpha)=1+\alpha>1.5\Rightarrow\alpha>0.5$; column 1: $\alpha+(1-\alpha)=1>0$ ✓; column 2: $0.5(1-\alpha)>0\Rightarrow\alpha<1$. So any $\alpha\in(0.5,1)$ works: yes. The quiz key (“Player 1 has no strictly dominated strategy”) counts pure-strategy domination only — when a question says “strictly dominated strategy” without “mixed”, follow the key’s convention but know this nuance.</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.plot({ xr: [0, 1], yr: [0, 3.2], xlab: 'Pr(R)', ylab: 'payoff', lines: [{ f: q => 3 * (1 - q), lab: 'U' }, { f: q => 3 * q, lab: 'M' }, { f: q => 1, lab: 'D' }, { f: q => 1.5, lab: '½U+½M', dash: true }], xt: [[0, '0'], [0.5, '½'], [1, '1']], yt: [[1, '1'], [1.5, '1.5'], [3, '3']], cap: 'D (flat at 1) lies below the dashed mixture everywhere — strictly dominated, though never below both U and M at once.' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Generalised in <a href="#/c/nbr">never-a-best-response</a> (Week 3); needs <a href="#/c/mixed-strategy">mixed strategy</a> notation.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Trying to dominate an action with a mixture that includes that action.</li><li>Using weak inequalities: domination by mixture must be strict in every column.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Lecture notes flag 3×2 “is D dominated?” as a classic. In PYQs it lurks inside the auction question; course assignment uses the BoS variant with action X.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'eq-intro', week: 1, title: 'The idea of equilibrium', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.7', text: '✓ Ch.2 §3', lec: '✓ W1_L8' }, prereq: ['dominance'],
  misShort: 'Thinking an equilibrium must be good for players or socially optimal.',
  intu: R`<p>An equilibrium is a <b>state of balance</b>: once there, nobody regrets their own choice given what others did, so nobody wants to move unilaterally. It is a prediction about stable play, not a recommendation of what is best for the group.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>All solution concepts (dominant-strategy equilibrium, Nash, subgame-perfect) are versions of “no unilateral regret”.</li><li>The PD shows equilibria can be inefficient — crucial for interpreting results.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>A profile $a^*$ is an <b>equilibrium</b> (in Nash’s sense) if for every player $i$, $u_i(a_i^*,a_{-i}^*)\ge u_i(a_i,a_{-i}^*)$ for all $a_i\in A_i$ — no player gains by changing <em>only</em> their own action.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Unilateral</b> means holding $a_{-i}^*$ fixed. Joint deviations (two players switching together, a diagonal move in the matrix) are not tested — that is why (C,C) in the PD is not an equilibrium even though both would like to move there together.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Check balance', q: R`<p>In the PD, is (Deny, Deny) an equilibrium?</p>`, s: R`<p>No: Ranga gains by switching to Confess ($0>-1$) while Billa keeps Deny.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Equilibrium vs optimum', q: R`<p>Traffic game (3,3),(0,4),(4,0),(1,1). Which profile is the equilibrium, which maximises total payoff?</p>`, s: R`<p>Equilibrium (D,D) — total 2. Social optimum (F,F) — total 6. Equilibrium ≠ optimum.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Multiple balances', q: R`<p>Coordination game: (A,A)=(2,2), (B,B)=(1,1), off-diagonal (0,0). List all pure equilibria.</p>`, s: R`<p>(A,A) and (B,B): at each, a unilateral switch gives 0. Equilibrium need not be unique; (B,B) is stable though Pareto-dominated.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Made precise in <a href="#/c/nash">Nash equilibrium</a>; justified in <a href="#/c/ne-interpret">interpretations</a>; refined in <a href="#/c/spe">SPE</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Equilibrium ≠ Pareto-efficient.</li><li>Equilibrium ≠ unique.</li><li>Equilibrium checks unilateral, not joint, deviations.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Conceptual MCQs: “No player can become better off by deviating unilaterally” is the defining phrase.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'assumptions', week: 1, title: 'Rationality, perfect recall & common knowledge', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §1.7.1–1.7.2', text: '✓ Ch.2 §3', lec: '✓ W1_L9, W2_L1a' }, prereq: ['prefs'],
  misShort: 'Confusing mutual knowledge (everyone knows X) with common knowledge (infinite hierarchy).',
  intu: R`<p>Game-theoretic predictions rest on assumptions about players: they have consistent preferences and maximise them (<b>rational</b>), remember what they knew and did (<b>perfect recall</b>), compute without error (<b>perfect calculators</b>), and the rules and rationality are <b>common knowledge</b>: I know, you know I know, I know you know I know … forever. The SMS story: two friends confirm a new meeting place by text; each confirmation adds one level of knowledge, but some level always remains uncertain — common knowledge is never reached by finite messages.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Each round of iterated elimination uses one more level of “knowing that others are rational”.</li><li>Backward induction relies on common knowledge of rationality.</li><li>Explains why real players deviate (e.g. the 2/3-average game).</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<ul><li><b>Rationality:</b> complete, transitive preferences; choose to maximise (expected) utility given beliefs.</li><li><b>Perfect recall:</b> never forgets own past moves or information.</li><li><b>Mutual knowledge</b> of X: everyone knows X. <b>Common knowledge</b> of X: everyone knows X, everyone knows that everyone knows X, and so on ad infinitum.</li></ul>`)}`,
  math: R`<p>Let $K_i X$ = “$i$ knows X”. Mutual knowledge: $K_1X\wedge K_2X$. Level 2: $K_1K_2X\wedge K_2K_1X$. Common knowledge: $K_{i_1}K_{i_2}\cdots K_{i_m}X$ for every finite sequence. In the SMS story, after Ranga’s message and Billa’s reply: $K_RX, K_BX, K_RK_BX, K_BK_RX$ hold, but Billa does not know that Ranga received the reply, so $K_BK_RK_BX$ fails.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Mutual vs common', q: R`<p>A teacher privately tells each of two students the exam date. Is it common knowledge?</p>`, s: R`<p>Mutual knowledge only: neither knows the other was told. Announcing it to both together makes it common knowledge.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Levels in IESDS', q: R`<p>In a 3-round IESDS, what knowledge does each round use?</p>`, s: R`<p>Round 1: players are rational. Round 2: each knows others are rational. Round 3: each knows that others know that everyone is rational. More rounds ⇒ deeper knowledge.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'SMS paradox', q: R`<p>Explain precisely where common knowledge fails after two messages.</p>`, s: R`<p>Ranga knows X; Billa knows X; Ranga knows Billa knows X (he got the reply); Billa knows Ranga knows X (he got the message). But Billa does not know whether his reply arrived, so “Billa knows that Ranga knows that Billa knows X” fails. Any finite chain of confirmations leaves the last sender uncertain.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Used by <a href="#/c/iesds">IESDS</a>, <a href="#/c/backward">backward induction</a>, <a href="#/c/ne-interpret">deductive interpretation of NE</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Rationality ≠ selfishness.</li><li>Dominant-strategy play needs only own rationality — not common knowledge.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Definitional MCQs (course assignment) and “what is needed for IESDS” style questions.</p>`)}` });

/* ---------------- Week 1 checkpoints ---------------- */
Q({ id: 'cp-decision-1', cp: 'decision', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`What turns a decision problem into a game?`, opts: ['There are at least two alternatives', 'Payoffs are measured in money', 'Each agent’s payoff depends on others’ choices too', 'Agents have conflicting interests'], ans: 2,
  sol: R`<p>Interdependence of payoffs is the defining feature. Conflict is not required (coordination games), money is irrelevant, and a single agent with many alternatives is still a decision problem.</p>`, why: ['Many alternatives can exist for a single agent.', 'Payoffs are utilities, not necessarily money.', 'Correct — strategic interdependence.', 'Games can have aligned interests.'], misc: 'Equating games with conflict.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-decision-2', cp: 'decision', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`Bill-splitting: values Cheap 1000 / Expensive 2500, prices 500 / 3000, bill split equally between two diners. What is your payoff if you order Expensive and your friend orders Cheap?`, ans: 750,
  sol: R`<p>Total bill $3000+500=3500$, you pay 1750. Payoff $2500-1750=750$.</p>`, misc: 'Charging yourself your own price instead of half the total.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-decision-3', cp: 'decision', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Economics marks for 0,1,2,3 hours: 7.0, 7.7, 8.2, 8.5. Geography: 6.0, 6.8, 7.4, 7.8. With 3 indivisible hours, how many hours should go to Geography?`, opts: ['0', '1', '2', '3'], ans: 2,
  sol: R`<p>Totals for (Econ, Geog): (3,0)=14.5, (2,1)=15.0, (1,2)=15.1, (0,3)=14.8. Best (1,2): 2 hours of Geography.</p>`, why: ['14.5 — worst except none.', '15.0 — close but lower.', 'Correct: 15.1.', '14.8.'], misc: 'Allocating all time to the subject with the highest first-hour gain.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-decision-4', cp: 'decision', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`Explain in 3–4 sentences why the bill-splitting arrangement makes both diners worse off than ordering alone.`,
  sol: R`<p>Under splitting, ordering Expensive costs you only half its price while you get its full value; the other half is paid by your friend (a negative externality). So E becomes strictly dominant for each (750 > 500 vs C; −500 > −750 vs E). Both order E and each gets −500, while (C,C) would give each +500 — a Prisoner’s Dilemma created by the cost-sharing rule.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-prefs-1', cp: 'prefs', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`When one is not able to compare two goods and is confused about which to choose, the preference violates:`, opts: ['Completeness', 'Reflexivity', 'Transitivity', 'All of them'], ans: 0,
  sol: R`<p>Completeness requires that any two outcomes can be ranked (≻, ≺ or ∼). Inability to compare violates it.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Reflexivity ($x\\succsim x$) is about comparing an item with itself.', 'Transitivity concerns three items.', 'Only completeness fails.'], src: 'GA', misc: 'Thinking indifference = incompleteness.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-prefs-2', cp: 'prefs', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`A person prefers tea to coffee, coffee to lassi and lassi to tea. The preference violates:`, opts: ['Completeness', 'Reflexivity', 'Transitivity', 'Convexity'], ans: 2,
  sol: R`<p>Tea ≻ coffee and coffee ≻ lassi should imply tea ≻ lassi; instead lassi ≻ tea — a cycle, violating transitivity.</p>`, why: ['Every pair is compared, so completeness holds.', 'Not about self-comparison.', 'Correct.', 'Convexity is about mixtures, not cycles.'], src: 'GA', misc: 'Mixing up the two axioms.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-prefs-3', cp: 'prefs', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Replacing every payoff of player 1 by its square root (all payoffs positive) can change which of the following?`, opts: ['The set of pure-strategy Nash equilibria', 'Which pure strategies are strictly dominated', 'Player 2’s equilibrium mixing probability in a mixed equilibrium', 'Nothing at all'], ans: 2,
  sol: R`<p>Square root is strictly increasing, so all pure comparisons (dominance, best responses, pure NE) are unchanged. But player 2’s mixing probability is chosen to make <em>player 1</em> indifferent, which depends on the cardinal values of player 1’s payoffs — it can change.</p>`, why: ['Ordinal transformations preserve pure NE.', 'Preserved: comparisons keep their sign.', 'Correct — mixed equilibria use cardinal (expected) utility.', 'Mixed probabilities can change.'], misc: 'Assuming payoffs are always only ordinal.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-prefs-4', cp: 'prefs', week: 1, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`Why can a person with cyclic preferences be exploited (“money pump”)? Construct a short example.`,
  sol: R`<p>Suppose tea ≻ coffee ≻ lassi ≻ tea. Holding lassi, they pay a small fee to switch to coffee (coffee ≻ lassi), pay again to switch to tea, then pay again to switch to lassi (lassi ≻ tea). They are back where they started, poorer — and can be cycled indefinitely. Transitivity rules this out.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-game-elements-1', cp: 'game-elements', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`In an $n$-player strategic game where player $i$ has $k_i$ strategies, the payoff function $u_i$ has domain of size and range respectively:`, opts: [R`$\prod_{i\in N}k_i$ and $\mathbb R$ (one real number)`, R`$n$ and $\mathbb R$`, R`$k_i$ and $\mathbb R^n$`, R`$2^{k_i}$ and $\mathbb R^2$`], ans: 0,
  sol: R`<p>$u_i:A_1\times\cdots\times A_n\to\mathbb R$; $|A|=\prod_i k_i$ profiles, each mapped to a single real payoff for player $i$.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Domain is profiles, not players.', 'Payoff depends on all players’ actions, not just $i$’s.', 'No power set is involved.'], misc: 'Thinking $u_i$ depends only on $i$’s own action.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-game-elements-2', cp: 'game-elements', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`What is NOT correct about the Prisoner’s Dilemma (Ranga & Billa) discussed in lecture?`, opts: ['Payoffs of both prisoners depend on each other’s actions', 'It is a one-shot game', 'Inspector Vinod is also a strategic player', 'None'], ans: 2,
  sol: R`<p>The inspector sets the rules but makes no strategic choice in the model, so he is not a player.</p>`, why: ['True: payoffs are interdependent.', 'True: played once.', 'Correct — this statement is false.', 'One statement is false.'], src: 'GA', misc: 'Listing every person in the story as a player.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-game-elements-3', cp: 'game-elements', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`Three players have 2, 3 and 4 actions. How many action profiles (cells across all matrices) does the game have?`, ans: 24, sol: R`<p>$2\times3\times4=24$.</p>`, misc: 'Adding instead of multiplying.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-game-elements-4', cp: 'game-elements', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`What is mechanism design and how does it relate to ordinary game theory?`,
  sol: R`<p>Ordinary game theory takes the rules as given and predicts outcomes (rules → equilibrium). Mechanism design reverses this: start from a desired outcome and design the rules (e.g. an auction format) so that rational, self-interested play in equilibrium produces that outcome. Hence “reverse game theory”.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-normal-form-1', cp: 'normal-form', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`In a bimatrix cell written (4, 1), the 4 is the payoff of:`, opts: ['The column player', 'The row player', 'Whoever moved first', 'Both players combined'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Convention: row player first.</p>`, why: ['Column payoff is second.', 'Correct.', 'Normal form has no move order.', 'Not a sum.'], misc: 'Reading payoffs in the wrong order.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-normal-form-2', cp: 'normal-form', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Auction: $v_1=3$, $v_2=5$, both bid 2, tie decided by a fair coin, winner pays bid. Payoff pair?`, opts: ['(1, 3)', '(0.5, 1.5)', '(0, 0)', '(1.5, 2.5)'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Each wins with probability ½: $u_1=\tfrac12(3-2)=0.5$, $u_2=\tfrac12(5-2)=1.5$.</p>`, why: ['That is the value if each won for sure.', 'Correct.', 'Ties still give expected surplus.', 'That is the (0,0) tie cell.'], misc: 'Ignoring the probability ½ in ties.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-normal-form-3', cp: 'normal-form', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which matrix is constant-sum?`, opts: ['(3,3) (0,4) / (4,0) (1,1)', '(80,20) (0,100) / (10,90) (60,40)', '(2,1) (0,0) / (0,0) (1,2)', '(1,1) (0,0) / (0,0) (0,0)'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Option B sums to 100 in every cell.</p>`, why: ['Sums 6,4,4,2.', 'Correct.', 'Sums 3,0,0,3.', 'Sums 2,0,0,0.'] });

Q({ id: 'cp-classify-1', cp: 'classify', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`A game in which every information set is a single node is a game of:`, opts: ['Complete information', 'Perfect information', 'Incomplete information', 'Imperfect information'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Singleton information sets ⇔ every mover knows the full history ⇔ perfect information.</p>`, why: ['Complete info concerns knowledge of payoffs.', 'Correct.', 'Opposite of complete.', 'Imperfect = some non-singleton set.'], misc: 'Perfect vs complete confusion.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-classify-2', cp: 'classify', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which statement is correct?`, opts: ['Every simultaneous-move game has perfect information', 'A game can have complete but imperfect information', 'Incomplete information means players move simultaneously', 'Perfect information implies a static game'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Simultaneous moves with known payoffs: complete (payoffs known) but imperfect (choices not observed).</p>`, why: ['Simultaneous play gives imperfect information.', 'Correct.', 'Incomplete concerns private payoff information.', 'Perfect information is a dynamic-game notion.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-classify-3', cp: 'classify', week: 1, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`Takeover: target value $x\in\{50,100\}$ equally likely, known only to the target, worth $1.5x$ to the acquirer; target accepts iff $y\ge x$. What is the acquirer’s expected payoff from the offer $y=100$?`, ans: 12.5,
  sol: R`<p>$y=100$ is accepted by both types. Payoff $=\tfrac12(75-100)+\tfrac12(150-100)=-12.5+25=12.5$. (Equal to offering 50, which gives $\tfrac12(75-50)=12.5$.)</p>`, misc: 'Forgetting that low-value targets also accept high offers.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-classify-4', cp: 'classify', week: 1, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Explain the adverse-selection logic in the takeover game with $x$ uniform on $\{0,\dots,100\}$: why is every positive bid unprofitable on average?`,
  sol: R`<p>A bid $y$ is accepted only by targets with $x\le y$; conditional on acceptance the average value is about $y/2$, worth $1.5\cdot y/2=0.75y$ to the acquirer, who pays $y$. Expected gain given acceptance ≈ $-0.25y<0$. The bid selects exactly the low-value firms (“winner’s curse”), so the optimal bid is 0 even though every firm is worth 50% more to the acquirer.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-coop-1', cp: 'coop', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`In a cooperative game of 4 players there are 16 possible (non-empty) coalitions.`, opts: ['True', 'False'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>$2^4=16$ subsets include the empty set; non-empty coalitions $=15$.</p>`, why: ['Counts the empty set.', 'Correct.'], src: 'GA', misc: 'Counting the empty set as a coalition.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-coop-2', cp: 'coop', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`Matching-shoes game (1 right shoe holder, 2 left shoe holders, pair worth 1). What is $v(\{1,2,3\})-v(\{1,2\})$?`, ans: 0, sol: R`<p>$1-1=0$: an extra left shoe adds nothing.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-coop-3', cp: 'coop', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`“Non-cooperative game” means:`, opts: ['Players have opposed interests', 'Players cannot communicate', 'Binding agreements are not assumed; the individual is the unit of analysis', 'Payoffs sum to zero'], ans: 2, sol: R`<p>Non-cooperative = no exogenous enforcement of agreements; analysis at the level of individual choices.</p>`, why: ['Interests can be aligned.', 'Cheap talk may be allowed.', 'Correct.', 'That is zero-sum.'] });

Q({ id: 'cp-pd-1', cp: 'pd', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`With $T$ (temptation), $R$ (reward), $P$ (punishment), $S$ (sucker), a symmetric 2×2 game is a Prisoner’s Dilemma iff:`, opts: ['$R>T>P>S$', '$T>R>P>S$', '$T>P>R>S$', '$R>P>T>S$'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>$T>R$ and $P>S$ make defection dominant; $R>P$ makes mutual defection inefficient.</p>`, why: ['If $R>T$, cooperation is a best reply to cooperation (Stag Hunt).', 'Correct.', 'If $P>R$, mutual defection is efficient — no dilemma.', 'Defection would not be dominant.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-pd-2', cp: 'pd', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`PD with Cooperate/Defect. Best response of player 1 against “Cooperate” of player 2 is:`, opts: ['Cooperate', 'Defect', 'None', 'Cannot decide'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>$T>R$: defecting against a cooperator pays more.</p>`, why: ['$R<T$.', 'Correct.', 'A best response exists.', 'Payoffs decide it.'], src: 'GA' });
Q({ id: 'cp-pd-3', cp: 'pd', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`Row payoffs: $R=6$, $S=0$, $P=2$, $T=x$. What is the smallest integer $x$ that makes the game a Prisoner’s Dilemma?`, ans: 7, sol: R`<p>Need $x>6$; smallest integer 7.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-pd-4', cp: 'pd', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Suppose Ranga cares about Billa: his utility is −(own years) − 0.5·(Billa’s years). Is Confess still dominant for Ranga?`,
  sol: R`<p>Ranga’s new payoffs: (C,C): −5 −2.5 = −7.5; (C,D): 0 − 10 = −10; (D,C): −20 − 0 = −20; (D,D): −1 −0.5 = −1.5. Against C: C (−7.5) > D (−20). Against D: D (−1.5) > C (−10). Confess is no longer dominant: Ranga now wants to match Billa. Altruism is handled by changing payoffs — game theory does not assume selfishness.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-dominance-1', cp: 'dominance', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Row payoffs: U = (2, 5, 3), D = (2, 4, 1) against L, C, R. Then:`, opts: ['U strictly dominates D', 'U weakly dominates D', 'D weakly dominates U', 'Neither dominates'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>$2=2$, $5>4$, $3>1$: ≥ everywhere with some > → weak dominance.</p>`, why: ['Tie against L rules out strict.', 'Correct.', 'D is never better.', 'U dominates weakly.'], misc: 'Ignoring a tie.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-dominance-2', cp: 'dominance', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which statement is true?`, opts: ['A strictly dominant strategy, if it exists, is unique', 'Every game has a dominant strategy for some player', 'A weakly dominant strategy must also be strictly dominant', 'If $a$ dominates $b$, then $a$ is a dominant strategy'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>If $a$ and $a'$ were both strictly dominant, each would be strictly better than the other — impossible.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Matching Pennies has none.', 'Reverse: strict ⇒ weak.', 'Must dominate all other strategies.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-dominance-3', cp: 'dominance', week: 1, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Auction with $v_1=4$, $v_2=4$, bids {0,1,2}, coin-toss ties, winner pays own bid. Which of player 1’s bids is strictly dominated by another pure bid?`, opts: ['Bid 0', 'Bid 1', 'Bid 2', 'None'], ans: 3,
  sol: R`<p>Player 1 payoffs vs $b_2=0,1,2$: bid 0: $(\tfrac12\cdot4,0,0)=(2,0,0)$; bid 1: $(3,\tfrac12\cdot3,0)=(3,1.5,0)$; bid 2: $(2,2,\tfrac12\cdot2)=(2,2,1)$.</p><ul><li>Bid 1 vs bid 0: $3>2$, $1.5>0$, but $0=0$ → only weak.</li><li>Bid 2 vs bid 0: $2=2$ (tie), $2>0$, $1>0$ → only weak.</li><li>Bid 1 vs bid 2: $3>2$ but $1.5<2$ → neither.</li></ul><p>No pure bid is <em>strictly</em> dominated by another pure bid: <b>None</b>. (Bid 0 is weakly dominated, and strictly dominated by the mixture ½·bid1 + ½·bid2 = (2.5, 1.75, 0.5).)</p>`, why: ['Bid 0 is only weakly dominated by pure bids (ties at one column each).', 'Bid 1 is the best reply to b₂=0.', 'Bid 2 is the best reply to b₂=1 and b₂=2.', 'Correct.'], misc: 'Declaring strict dominance despite a tie.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-dominance-4', cp: 'dominance', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`Why does dominant-strategy reasoning require only a player’s own rationality, not knowledge of others’ rationality?`,
  sol: R`<p>A dominant strategy is optimal against <em>every</em> possible profile of the others, so the player does not need any belief about what others will do or whether they are rational. Only “I maximise my payoff” is used.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-mixed-dom-1', cp: 'mixed-dom', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Row payoffs vs L, R: U = (4, 0), M = (0, 4), D = (1.5, 1.5). Is D strictly dominated?`, opts: ['Yes, by U', 'Yes, by ½U + ½M', 'No, it is never dominated', 'Only weakly by M'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>½U+½M gives (2, 2) > (1.5, 1.5). No pure row dominates D.</p>`, why: ['U gives 0 < 1.5 against R.', 'Correct.', 'A mixture does it.', 'M gives 0 < 1.5 against L.'], misc: 'Stopping after checking pure strategies.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-mixed-dom-2', cp: 'mixed-dom', week: 1, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`Row payoffs vs L, R: U=(3,0), M=(0,3), D=(1,1). $D$ is strictly dominated by $\alpha U+(1-\alpha)M$ iff $\alpha$ lies in an open interval $(a,b)$. Enter $b-a$.`, ans: 1 / 3, tol: 0.01, ansText: '1/3 (interval (1/3, 2/3))',
  sol: R`<p>vs L: $3\alpha>1\Rightarrow\alpha>1/3$. vs R: $3(1-\alpha)>1\Rightarrow\alpha<2/3$. Width $2/3-1/3=1/3$.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-mixed-dom-3', cp: 'mixed-dom', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which can never happen?`, opts: ['A pure action not dominated by any pure action is dominated by a mixture', R`A mixture $\tfrac12a+\tfrac12b$ strictly dominates both $a$ and $b$`, 'A strictly dominated action is also weakly dominated', 'A pure action is dominated by a mixture of two other actions'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>In each column the mixture pays the average of $a$’s and $b$’s payoffs, which cannot exceed the larger of the two. So it cannot beat both $a$ and $b$ in every column. (It <em>can</em> beat $a$ alone — e.g. whenever $b$ itself strictly dominates $a$.)</p>`, why: ['Possible — the lecture 3×2 example.', 'Correct: an average never exceeds both components.', 'Always true (strict ⇒ weak).', 'Possible — same example.'], misc: 'Thinking an average can beat all of its components.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-mixed-dom-4', cp: 'mixed-dom', week: 1, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`In the PYQ auction (values 3, 5), show that $b_1=0$ is strictly dominated by a mixed strategy, and explain why the official key nevertheless says player 1 has no strictly dominated strategy.`,
  sol: R`<p>$b_1=0$ gives (1.5, 0, 0). Mix $0.75\cdot(2,1,0)+0.25\cdot(1,1,0.5)=(1.75,1,0.125)$ > (1.5, 0, 0) in every column. So it is dominated by a mixture. The quiz asks about strictly dominated strategies in the pure-strategy sense (comparison with other pure bids); no pure bid of player 1 strictly beats another in every column, so the keyed answer is “Player 1”. Know both views; follow the key’s pure-strategy convention unless mixtures are mentioned.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-eq-intro-1', cp: 'eq-intro', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which phrase best defines an equilibrium (Nash)?`, opts: ['Total payoff is maximised', 'No player can gain by unilaterally changing their action', 'All players get equal payoffs', 'No group of players can gain by jointly deviating'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Unilateral no-regret.</p>`, why: ['That is social optimality.', 'Correct.', 'Fairness is irrelevant.', 'That is a coalition-proofness idea, stronger than Nash.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-eq-intro-2', cp: 'eq-intro', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Game: (A,A)=(2,2), (A,B)=(0,0), (B,A)=(0,0), (B,B)=(1,1). How many pure equilibria?`, opts: ['0', '1', '2', '4'], ans: 2, sol: R`<p>(A,A) and (B,B).</p>`, why: ['Both diagonal cells are stable.', 'Missing (B,B), which is stable though inferior.', 'Correct.', 'Off-diagonals are not stable.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-eq-intro-3', cp: 'eq-intro', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`Give an example showing that an equilibrium can be Pareto-dominated by another outcome, and explain why the better outcome is not reached.`,
  sol: R`<p>PD: equilibrium (Confess, Confess) = (−5,−5) is worse for both than (Deny, Deny) = (−1,−1). From (D,D) each player gains by switching alone to Confess (0 > −1), so (D,D) is not stable; without binding agreements the players cannot hold each other to it.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-assumptions-1', cp: 'assumptions', week: 1, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`“Everyone knows X, everyone knows that everyone knows X, and so on ad infinitum” defines:`, opts: ['Mutual knowledge', 'Common knowledge', 'Perfect recall', 'Complete information'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>The infinite hierarchy is common knowledge.</p>`, why: ['Only the first level.', 'Correct.', 'About remembering one’s own past.', 'About payoffs being known.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-assumptions-2', cp: 'assumptions', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which solution method requires NO knowledge of the other players’ rationality?`, opts: ['Iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies (2+ rounds)', 'Backward induction in a 3-stage game', 'Playing a strictly dominant strategy', 'Predicting a unique Nash equilibrium'], ans: 2, sol: R`<p>A dominant strategy is optimal regardless of others’ behaviour.</p>`, why: ['Round 2 uses “I know you are rational”.', 'Uses common knowledge of rationality.', 'Correct.', 'Requires correct beliefs about others.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-assumptions-3', cp: 'assumptions', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`In the SMS meeting story, why would adding a third message (Ranga confirms Billa’s reply) still not create common knowledge?`,
  sol: R`<p>The third message tells Billa that Ranga received the reply, adding one more level, but now Ranga does not know whether his confirmation arrived. Each message closes one level and opens uncertainty at the next; common knowledge needs every finite level, which no finite exchange over an unreliable channel achieves.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-normal-form-4', cp: 'normal-form', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Explain how player 1 and player 2 read a bimatrix differently when looking for their best responses.`, sol: R`<p>Player 1 fixes a column (player 2’s action) and compares the <em>first</em> numbers down that column. Player 2 fixes a row and compares the <em>second</em> numbers along that row. Each player only ever compares their own payoffs, holding the other’s action fixed.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-coop-4', cp: 'coop', week: 1, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`In the matching-shoes game (one right shoe, two left shoes), why does the right-shoe owner have more bargaining power?`, sol: R`<p>Every valuable coalition must include the right-shoe owner, while the two left-shoe owners are substitutes for each other: $v(\{1,2\})=v(\{1,3\})=1$, $v(\{2,3\})=0$. Each left-shoe owner’s marginal contribution to a coalition that already has the other is 0, so they compete for player 1, who can capture most of the surplus.</p>` });
